\pard\sa60\f1\fs30\b\cf1 Reviewer QgDY\b0\par
\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp40\cf4\par
\pard\sb80\sa50\f0\fs17\b\cf2 ORIGINAL REVIEW\b0\par
\pard\sb0\sa80\sl220\slmult1\f0\fs17\cf2 Official Review of Submission29735 by Reviewer QgDY\line Official Reviewby Reviewer QgDY24 Jun 2026, 20:00 (modified: 23 Jul 2026, 12:43)Program Chairs, Senior Area Chairs, Area Chairs, Reviewers Submitted, Authors, Reviewer QgDYRevisions\line Summary:\line The paper approximates single neurons as conductance-based dendritic networks to tackle the problem of how neurons could implement credit assignment without backprop. In this conductance-based network, they derive the 'conductance-stage path gain'. They factorize the synaptic gradient in terms of presynaptic activity, driving force, and input resistance, and one non-local compartment error. They then perform a series of experiments with MNIST, Fashion-MNIST data to highlight the regimes where shunting modulated input resistance can make localCA better.\par
\pard\sb0\sa80\sl220\slmult1\f0\fs17\cf2 Contribution Type: General: Most submissions will fall into this type.\line Strengths And Weaknesses:\line Strengths\line The topic is interesting and important for neuroAI.\line There is not much work in my knowledge that incorporates the role of morphology in credit assignment and plasticity, and novel theoretical frameworks might be needed with new datasets that are able to image local dendritic activity and plasticity.\line Weaknesses\line Paper is very hard to follow. Specific comments below:\line The abstract, introduction and model derivation introduce a lot of jargon and unclear terminology. e.g. \u8216?identity upward transfer\u8217?, \u8216?localCA\u8217? in the abstract, \u8216?E/I synapse banks\u8217?, \u8216?pathway-broadcast mixing weight\u8217?, \u8216?depth-dependent modulation in morphology extensions\u8217?. It took me a couple of reads for context to understand what they mean. I see that a lot of these terms are properly elaborated in the appendix - so either the authors provide an intuitive, abbreviated understanding of these in the main text or link to the relevant section in the appendix.\line The simplifying assumptions are not elaborated on, or explained well.\line Figure captions are inadequate, and the figures are hard to interpret.\line Paper organization and structuring is not great. I could not follow the link between the theoretical derivations, the implications to local learning rules (subsequent section) and then the experiments section. The flow needs to be improved quite a bit in order for the paper to be intelligible. Perhaps the authors could move some details of derivations to the appendix, and elaborate on the background, study rationale etc instead? I see that a lot of those might have been relegated to the appendix. But I feel like readers should be able to follow the paper without reading the appendix in detail.\par
\pard\sb0\sa80\sl220\slmult1\f0\fs17\cf2 Perhaps due to the structure and writing of the paper, the significance of the study and future applications are not very clear to me. I see that there is an appendix table S5 listed in the Discussions and Limitations section that points to biological predictions; I\u8217?d have appreciated a simplified version of that somewhere in the main text.\par
\pard\sb0\sa80\sl220\slmult1\f0\fs17\cf2 Weirdly, the limitations of the paper, in terms of the biological plausibility assumptions, are relegated to a subsection in the appendix. This should be in the main text. Throughout my read of the paper, I was looking for this section in order to understand how to build on their model - see my question below.\par
\pard\sb0\sa80\sl220\slmult1\f0\fs17\cf2 Quality: 2: not good\line Clarity: 1: poor\line Significance: 3: good\line Originality: 2: not good\line Questions:\line How would the authors propose that the limitations around their framework that uses passive cable equations rather than temporal membrane dynamics, be addressed? Do they expect these to have second-order effects?\par
\pard\sb0\sa80\sl220\slmult1\f0\fs17\cf2 Related to the question above, Are the dynamic effects in this conductance-based models, that boil down to changes in local conductances, decidedly first order? Can there be other non-linear effects that operate on longer timescales, such as calcium and neuromodulatory dynamics, that can be build onto this work? How might those be taken into consideration?\par
\pard\sb0\sa80\sl220\slmult1\f0\fs17\cf2 To what extent is this framework relevant to real neuronal morphologies? Is this work supposed to give a general idea of how branching morphology features into local credit-assignment, and so minute differences in branching patterns won\u8217?t cause significant differences across neurons of the same cell type or across cell types? Suppose I wanted to study the differences in local credit assignment across morphologies, would this framework be relevant?\par
\pard\sb0\sa80\sl220\slmult1\f0\fs17\cf2 Limitations:\line Limitations were somewhat addressed but not entirely; key limitations were relegated to the appendix. See comment 3 under \u8216?Weaknesses\u8217?\par
\pard\sb0\sa80\sl220\slmult1\f0\fs17\cf2 Rating: 2: Reject: For instance, a paper with technical flaws, weak evaluation, inadequate reproducibility and incompletely addressed ethical considerations.\line Confidence: 2: You are willing to defend your assessment, but it is quite likely that you did not understand the central parts of the submission or that you are unfamiliar with some pieces of related work. Math/other details were not carefully checked.\line Ethical Concerns: NO or VERY MINOR ethics concerns only\line Paper Formatting Concerns:\line Did not notice anything.\par
\pard\sb0\sa80\sl220\slmult1\f0\fs17\cf2 Code Of Conduct Acknowledgement: Yes\line Responsible Reviewing Acknowledgement: Yes\par
\pard\sb80\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp40\cf4\par
\pard\sb100\sa60\f0\fs24\b\cf3 AUTHOR RESPONSE\b0\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 Thank you for emphasizing that the theory-to-experiment chain must be understandable without reconstructing it from the appendix. We took this concern seriously and reorganized the argument around one simple question: what exact credit does a dendritic synapse require, and how much of it can a biologically restricted teaching signal provide?\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 Bottom line. The clearer organization reveals three strong quantitative results. First, the derived factorization reconstructs autograd with relative error below 1.9e-7. Second, exact path transport lets the theorem-derived 3F rule reach 97.18 to 97.21%, matching backpropagation at 97.03%. Third, dense compartment-wise feedback is not necessary in the regular tree: one teaching coordinate shared across each modeled neuron's 13 compartments raises 3F accuracy to 97.15% in shunting and 97.14% in additive networks, with improvement in every one of 15 paired seeds. The paper now presents these results as a direct mechanistic chain rather than as a collection of diagnostics.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 The paper in four statements\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 1. A synaptic gradient in the modeled dendritic tree is exactly a local sensitivity multiplied by a compartment-specific error.\line 2. The local sensitivity uses presynaptic activity, synaptic driving force, and local input resistance.\line 3. The compartment error is a somatic error transported through the branches between that synapse and the soma.\line 4. Shunting changes input resistance and therefore regulates the gain of selected credit paths; restricted-feedback experiments test whether enough of the required error survives for learning.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 Exact-gradient reconstruction tests statements 1 through 3. Path-gain, inhibition-intervention, morphology, feedback, and transported-error controls test statement 4. Accuracy is the learning consequence of this chain, not a substitute for the derivation.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 1. Presentation and organization\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 The revision begins with a dendritic-tree schematic and voltage equation, then defines eligibility, compartment error, and feedback. Results follow five steps: exact factorization, feedback fidelity, conductance interventions, task competence, and rule/feedback controls. Assumptions, predictions, and limitations are now in the main discussion; proofs and secondary sweeps remain in the supplement.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 We removed terms such as \u8220?synapse banks,\u8221? \u8220?identity upward transfer,\u8221? and \u8220?gold-ringed soma,\u8221? define all abbreviations before use, and rebuilt figures with non-overlapping elements and standalone captions.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 Every diagnostic now defines its axes: the 4F proxy estimates covariance over examples after reducing non-batch coordinates; path-gain CV is over compartments within examples; SVD uses examples by compartments; and gradient metrics compare matched parameter vectors. The 5F residual term is now a bounded predictability factor, not confidence.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 2. Feedback correction produces a stronger learning result\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 The original `per_soma` alias preserves the soma vector only at matching width and otherwise uses one scalar; it is now called matched-width/scalar-fallback feedback. An unconstrained rank-1 SVD does not test this uniform broadcast, so pooled SVD and field metrics are no longer used to claim shunting-specific compatibility.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 The important result is what happens when neuron identity is preserved. Repeating one soma coordinate over the 13 compartments of each modeled neuron raises 3F MNIST from 90.75% to 97.15% in shunting and from 91.58% to 97.14% in additive networks. The gains are 6.40 and 5.56 percentage points, occur in all 15 pairs, and have signed-rank p=6.1e-5. Changing only the diagnostic feedback field raises branch-gradient cosine from 0.007 to 0.667 and from 0.024 to 0.599, again in every pair.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 A five-seed exact-transport factorial provides the ceiling: 3F reaches 97.18 to 97.21%, 5F 97.12 to 97.20%, and backpropagation 97.03%. Thus one coordinate per neuron captures almost all of dense transport's benefit: preserve neuron identity and let ancestry supply the routing map.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 3. Significance and positive shunting evidence\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 The significance is that conductance and morphology enter the exact credit equation in a measurable and experimentally testable way. For inhibitory conductance at compartment k,\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 `d log(alpha_n) / d G_k^I = -R_k`\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 for every descendant n whose path traverses k, with no corresponding path effect on unrelated branches. This predicts a spatially selective change in plasticity signals, with strength set by local input resistance and route geometry.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 Positive regimes are quantitative. At N_I=5, path-gain CV is about 80% lower under shunting, with the same ordering in all five pairs. Under restricted 5F feedback, shunting exceeds additive on MNIST (91.1 versus 89.4%), Fashion-MNIST (83.8 versus 83.1%), figure-ground MNIST (80.3 versus 78.7%), and compact CIFAR-10 (29.7 versus 25.5%). Across one to four dendritic layers, shunting changes from 63.5 to 57.4%, while additive changes from 54.9 to 29.7%. Shunting is stable through broadcast-noise sigma=0.1, while additive reaches chance at sigma=1.0.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 These results support a regime-dependent claim. A matched initialization factorial finds no universal 3F architecture advantage, and neuron-wise feedback brings both cores to about 97.15%. The positive conclusion is therefore not \u8220?shunting always wins,\u8221? but that shunting gives a mathematically defined, branch-selective control of credit and improves learning in deeper, noisier, and inhibition-sensitive restricted-feedback regimes.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 The main text now predicts that a focal shunt preferentially alters descendant plasticity, scales with input resistance, and affects route families according to proximal/distal location and task relevance. These connect voltage, inhibition, morphology, and plasticity measurements.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 4. Passive equilibrium and temporal dynamics\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 The theorem is steady-state, not a claim that time is unimportant. Capacitance, spikes, channel kinetics, calcium, neuromodulation, and recurrence add state and may change credit substantially. A temporal extension replaces the path product with an adjoint or eligibility trace; the current result is its equilibrium starting point.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 Local nonlinearities enter path gain through their derivatives. Replacing parametric tanh by identity gives 90.81+/-0.33% versus 90.88+/-0.77% across five seeds, so competence is not created by the selected nonlinearity. A 15-seed identity-transfer control also separates gradient direction from final learning.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 Active calcium or sodium conductances can amplify or gate credit; calcium may create branch eligibility traces and neuromodulation may scale or consolidate them. These are direct extensions, not completed experiments.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 5. Relevance to real morphology\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 The derivation permits arbitrary depth, asymmetric branching, and branch-specific conductances. Geometric differences matter when they alter resistance or task-relevant routes. Multiple parents introduce a path sum; recurrence requires an adjoint.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 The depth experiment already shows route-length sensitivity. All 40 checkpoints retain every stage (minimum stage-median coupling 0.716), excluding stage pruning. The morphology grid remains descriptive because some couplings initialize near a floor.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 For reconstructed cells, one can insert the parent-child graph and fitted conductances, compute path gains and feedback fields, and compare credit routing across morphologies. The code already supports arbitrary rooted trees, E/I interventions, exact-gradient checks, and multiple feedback families.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 6. Limitations in the main argument\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 The main discussion now states steady-state non-spiking dynamics, a rooted tree, nonnegative conductances, input-driven inhibition, and access to a soma teaching signal. It separates theorem terms from reactivation, decoder choices, top-k selection, 4F/5F preconditioning, and auxiliary losses.\par
\pard\sb0\sa140\sl280\slmult1\f0\fs23\cf3 The supported conclusion is positive and precise: conductance and branching determine the exact geometry of dendritic credit; the local 3F rule reaches the backpropagation ceiling when feedback preserves the required structure; neuron-indexed feedback achieves this with one coordinate per modeled neuron; and shunting supplies an exact, spatially selective control mechanism with measurable benefits in defined learning regimes. The passive model is therefore a tractable foundation for temporal and reconstructed-neuron work, not merely a simplified classifier.\par
\pard\sb80\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp40\cf4\par
}